• Use the object Exporter 
  for providing and exporting services. It represents a group of people, whose
individual members you cannot distinguish and whom you cannot address as individuals.
• Use the object FootPath 
  for modeling a path on which the Worker walks from the WorkerPool to the
Workplace.
• Use the object LockoutZone 
  for controlling a group of material flow objects. If one of these objects
fails, all other objects within the lockout zone stop processing parts
• Use the object Marker 
  for setting milestones along which the AGVs drive from the AGVPool to their
destination.
• Use the object ShiftCalendar 
  for modeling a shift work system in your plant.
• Use the object Worker 
  for doing a job on a workplace at a station.
The toolbar Resources does not show the Worker. You can access the class of the Worker in the Class
Library.
• Use the object WorkerPool 
  for modeling the staff room of your plant.
• Use the object Workplace 
  for modeling the actual place attached to the station, where the Worker
performs his job.
Do not confuse the Plant Simulation resource objects with the Plant Simulation material flow resources.
The material flow resources are any of the built-in Material Flow Objects, Fluid Objects or a Frame in
which you modeled a machine.
Workplace
Workplace
Use the object Workplace for modeling the actual place at the station, where the Worker performs his job.
Image
Description
The Worker-WorkerPool-Workplace-FootPath-concept refines the Broker-Importer-Exporter-concept. You
can assign a Workplace 
  to the material flow objects that support an Importer. In this case, the
importers are processing or repairing importers.
For the transport importer, who controls how the Workers carry parts, you can assign a Workplace to the
Buffer, the PlaceBuffer, the Source, the Sorter, and the Store in addition.
Workplace
11-2972 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The Workplace is the actual place at the station where the Worker performs his job.
Note
You can, but you do not have to assign a Workplace to a station, i.e., a material flow object.
If you do not use a FootPath, Plant Simulation transports the Worker directly to the Workplace. Here the
Worker functions as an Exporter with a capacity of 1.
The Workers are created in the WorkerPool and they stay there when they do not work and are waiting for
a job order.
The Broker procures the Worker for the individual work stations. As soon as a Worker can provide a service,
the Broker sends him from his WorkerPool to the work station that ordered him:
• If the Worker walks freely within the area of the simulation model, he walks on the shortest route to the
Workplace and walks around any obstacles you designated.
The Worker consumes time, when he walks freely within the area to the Workplace.
• If you modeled the work station using one of the material flow objects and the Workplace and
connected the WorkerPool and the Workplace with a FootPath, Plant Simulation animates the Worker
while he moves on the FootPath and while he works on the Workplace.
The Worker consumes time when he walks on the FootPath.
If several Workers walk to the same Workplace and have to wait their turn before they can step onto it,
you can define how they queue up in front of the workplace.
• If you activated Beam to workplace and if you inserted a Workplace, and the Worker, who is supposed
to work there, cannot reach it via a FootPath, Plant Simulation beams the Worker directly to the
Workplace and animates him there.
• If you activated Workers can work remotely, if you did not insert a Workplace, if no Workplace exists
that supports this service, or if all Workplaces that support this service are occupied, Plant Simulation
seemingly transports the Worker to the work station itself. He then does his job at the work station.
Plant Simulation does not animate him at the work station though, but continues to animate him in the
WorkerPool.
To show a tooltip with information about the Workplace, hover with the mouse over it.
Workplace
